WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.972
Firmware often participates in device
identity.

2
00:00:03.052 --> 00:00:06.348
It may store identifiers, keys,
certificates, pairing material,

3
00:00:06.428 --> 00:00:08.782
manufacturing records, service
credentials,

4
00:00:08.862 --> 00:00:12.394
or counters that help the device prove
what it is or decide what it may do.

5
00:00:12.474 --> 00:00:15.711
These values need lifecycle ownership, not
just a place in flash.

6
00:00:15.791 --> 00:00:18.557
Each key or secret should have a clear
purpose and scope.

7
00:00:18.637 --> 00:00:22.522
A signing trust anchor, a device
authentication key, a transport credential,

8
00:00:22.602 --> 00:00:26.074
a pairing secret, and a manufacturing
token are not interchangeable.

9
00:00:26.154 --> 00:00:29.627
Naming the purpose helps reviewers decide
where the value may be used,

10
00:00:29.707 --> 00:00:32.120
who may access it, and how it should be
protected.

11
00:00:32.200 --> 00:00:34.436
Provisioning is part of the security
design.

12
00:00:34.516 --> 00:00:38.578
Values may be installed during
manufacturing, personalization, field service,

13
00:00:38.658 --> 00:00:40.011
first boot, or enrollment.

14
00:00:40.091 --> 00:00:43.446
The process should define who creates the
value, where it is stored,

15
00:00:43.526 --> 00:00:47.646
how it is protected in transit, what
records are kept, and how mistakes are handled.

16
00:00:47.726 --> 00:00:51.081
Hard-coded secrets are risky because they
are difficult to rotate,

17
00:00:51.161 --> 00:00:54.692
easy to copy across builds, and hard to
manage across product variants.

18
00:00:54.772 --> 00:00:58.892
Firmware should avoid embedding shared
secret material directly in source code or

19
00:00:58.972 --> 00:01:00.385
widely distributed images.

20
00:01:00.465 --> 00:01:04.232
When a device needs identity, use a
reviewed provisioning and storage plan.

21
00:01:04.312 --> 00:01:07.784
Storage controls should match sensitivity
and platform capability.

22
00:01:07.864 --> 00:01:10.925
Some devices have secure elements,
protected flash regions,

23
00:01:11.005 --> 00:01:14.595
one-time programmable memory, hardware key
slots, or access controls.

24
00:01:14.675 --> 00:01:18.148
Others have fewer protections and need
compensating design choices.

25
00:01:18.228 --> 00:01:22.230
The important point is to document the
assumption and test that the release build

26
00:01:22.310 --> 00:01:22.898
matches it.

27
00:01:22.978 --> 00:01:25.804
Logging and diagnostics must handle
secrets carefully.

28
00:01:25.884 --> 00:01:29.827
A helpful debug line during bring-up can
become an exposure in a field build if it

29
00:01:29.907 --> 00:01:33.615
records keys, credentials, tokens,
certificates with private material,

30
00:01:33.695 --> 00:01:35.049
or sensitive identifiers.

31
00:01:35.129 --> 00:01:39.072
Logs should be reviewed for what they
reveal, not only for whether they compile.

32
00:01:39.152 --> 00:01:42.095
Lifecycle planning includes replacement
and retirement.

33
00:01:42.175 --> 00:01:45.000
Teams should know what happens if a
certificate expires,

34
00:01:45.080 --> 00:01:48.729
a manufacturing process changes, a service
credential must be replaced,

35
00:01:48.809 --> 00:01:50.869
or a product line needs a new trust
anchor.

36
00:01:50.949 --> 00:01:55.069
Firmware security is stronger when
identity has an owner from design through field

37
00:01:55.149 --> 00:01:55.620
support.
